  "instructions": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "primary_message": "Rub salt, pepper, and herbs all over meat. Depending on the size of your roast, you will need about 1 1/2 to 2 teaspoons of salt and pepper, each. Roll and tie up roast into a cylinder. The roast will benefit from being salted and kept in the refrigerator overnight, or you can proceed with the recipe immediately.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "primary_message": "Preheat oven to 325°F. In a Dutch oven, heat 2 tablespoons of oil or lard. Add tied up roast and brown it on all sides, about 10 minutes total. 5 minutes into the browning, add chopped garlic and let it brown alongside meat.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "primary_message": "Remove meat from pot and pour off all but 1 teaspoon of fat. Add milk, bay leaves, lemon juice, and zest. Bring mixture to a boil. Return roast to pot, placing skin side up. Cover pot and braise in oven for 1 hour. Remove cover and braise for 30 minutes longer, or until internal temperature of roast registers around 160 (71°C) to 170°F. The skin on the roast should be golden brown and crisp.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "primary_message": "Remove roast from pot and place it into a sauté pan or hotel pan. Place roast under broiler for 30 seconds to 1 minute, to further crisp up skin.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "primary_message": "In the meantime, reduce sauce: Take braising milk in pot and place it on top of stove. Simmer sauce for 5 to 7 minutes, until sauce has formed light-brown curds. Remove bay leaves.",
      "secondary_message": null
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "primary_message": "Slice as much of the roast as you need into 1/2-inch thick slices, arrange on a platter, and spoon sauce over them. Serve immediately. Leftovers may be refrigerated and sliced cold for use in sandwiches.",
      "secondary_message": null
    }
  ],
